WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
Good to have you on SpaceX Daily, Episode 25, coming to you from Vancouver.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:10.860
It's July 7, 2026. From Starbase to Orbit, here's your SpaceX rundown.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:16.960
Record Falcon Cadence and a fresh ride-share deployment show how reuse margins are widening the gap

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:23.740
between annual launch capacity and demand. SpaceX has already surpassed its prior first half-launch

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:30.320
totals with 82 missions completed through early July. The pace reflects continued booster reuse

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:36.240
gains with multiple vehicles now exceeding 30 flights. This directly extends the tracked

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:42.360
Falcon launch cadence and reuse arc by testing whether pad and range throughput can sustain the

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:48.180
current rate through year end. Next milestones to watch are the cumulative booster flight count

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:55.200
and any new turnaround records at SLC 40 or Vandenberg. The same reuse margins that enabled

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.520
the record also created excess capacity now being filled by high-volume ride shares.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:09.660
A Falcon 9 lifted off from Vandenberg pad 4E at 0712 UTC carrying 81 small satellites into

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:16.260
sun synchronous orbit. Deployment occurred over a 2.5 hour window after the second stage burns.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:22.160
The mission continues the pattern of high-volume ride shares that fill excess capacity created

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:28.520
by rapid Falcon reuse. Observers will track whether the next Vandenberg flight maintains the same

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:34.680
multi-hour deployment sequence. Battery and attitude control margins now stand as the next

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:41.700
engineering checkpoint for repeated long-coast profiles. The upcoming Starlink 10-42 mission from

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:51.760
SLC 40 is scheduled for a July 9 launch window opening at 0905 UTC. Booster B1067 is slated for

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:59.800
its 36th flight with recovery on the drone ship ASOG. The thread notes a 3-day 22-hour pad turnaround

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:07.220
since the prior SLC 40 use. Listeners following Starlink constellation growth can watch for confirmation

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:14.640
of the 186th consecutive successful launch if the flight succeeds. While Falcon keeps setting

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.800
cadence marks, Starbase hardware teams are preparing the next integrated test campaign.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:26.180
A SpaceX satellite secured a military contract round that drew congressional attention over the

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:32.980
balance between rapid capability and open competition. The award occurs while the Pentagon accelerates

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:38.980
proliferated low-earth orbit networks. This development sits at the intersection of star-shield

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:45.240
hardware and national security launch demand. Watch for any follow-on policy language on source

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:51.080
selection criteria. National security demand is another growing driver of launch cadence.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:57.860
Ground cameras captured ongoing Starbase vehicle movements and tower preparations on July 6th.

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:03.260
The footage shows continued stacking and subsystem checkout activity at the Texas site.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:08.860
These updates track the hardware cadence that supports the next Starship flight test window.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:14.440
The next visible milestones are expected to be ship or booster static fire attempts.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:20.960
Starships lunar ambitions depend on closing several key performance gaps before crude flights.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.940
Updated timelines for the Starship human landing system show the sequence of uncrewed

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:34.040
demonstrations required before crude lunar landings. The architecture still hinges on orbital refueling

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:40.140
and heat shield performance. This continues the Starship program arc by clarifying how many

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:46.560
tanker flights are baseline for each HLS mission. Observers will monitor whether the next integrated

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:53.040
test flight closes any of the remaining refueling gaps. Ground infrastructure is also being upgraded

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:59.740
to support higher Raptor production rates. SpaceX is advancing plans for an onsite natural gas

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:06.220
pipeline to supply methane feedstock at Starbase. The infrastructure would support higher Raptor

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:12.040
production and test rates by reducing trucked propellant logistics. This ties into the Raptor

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:16.120
engine program by addressing a ground support constraint on engine throughput.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:23.140
The next visible step is any permit or construction timeline update. The Florida Manifest lists a

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:29.180
single Falcon 9 mission from Cape Canaveral this week. The flight aligns with the ongoing cadence

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:35.820
that has already set new half year records. This update reinforces the tracked Falcon launch cadence

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:41.000
and reuse question of how many additional flights the eastern range can absorb without

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:46.840
extending turnarounds. The next published schedule revision will indicate whether a second Florida

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:53.620
slot opens. Lab Padre stream viewers noted new Starbase tower arm movements and additional ground

00:04:53.620 --> 00:05:00.080
equipment staging near the launch mount. Observers on the thread interpreted the activity as preparation for

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:07.020
an upcoming integrated vehicle static fire. Florida launch spotting accounts reported the Vandenberg

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:13.660
ride share liftoff visible on marine radar feeds despite the midnight local time. Several users

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:20.820
posted timestamped images of the plume from coastal vantage points are as SpaceX commenters compiled

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:26.660
a running list of the 81 payloads on the recent ride share highlighting the mix of technology

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:33.200
demonstration and commercial CubeSats. Worth keeping an eye on is the analysis arguing that Starship

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:39.220
development costs and flight cadence requirements are diverting resources from near term lunar lander

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:46.180
alternatives potentially delaying US crude moon landings. The piece frames the architectures dependence

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:53.040
on rapid orbital refueling as a schedule risk. Resolution would require either demonstrated refueling

00:05:53.040 --> 00:06:00.280
success or a parallel lander program funded at scale. On the AI front, SpaceX disclosed plans for

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:06.220
a star-mind AI satellite constellation that would place processing nodes in orbit and route traffic

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:13.220
over laser inter-satellite links. The architecture aims to move inference workloads closer to data sources

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:20.160
while leveraging existing starlink laser hardware. A satirical report claiming SpaceX had absorbed

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:27.720
XAI and rebranded as SpaceX AI circulated but carried no corroborating details from either company.

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:34.220
From an engineering standpoint, the recent Midnight Falcon 9 ride share from Vandenberg placed 81

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:40.420
spacecraft into Sun synchronous orbit through a sequence of two second stage burns separated by a

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:46.720
coast phase. The limiting factor is not thrust but the propellant mass that must be carried to perform

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:53.000
the plane change and circularization burns after the initial insertion. Each additional kilogram

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:58.480
of payload therefore requires either more propellant or a more efficient trajectory that still

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:05.060
satisfies the customer's orbital elements. SpaceX's solution has been to standardize the deployment

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:11.740
profile so the second stage can release satellites over a 2.5 hour window while the stage itself

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:18.500
remains in a stable attitude. This approach trades a modest increase in battery and attitude control

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:23.520
propellant for the ability to serve many distinct orbital planes from a single launch.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:29.380
The resulting idiot index for the upper stage mission is lowered because the same hardware that

00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:36.180
flies 30 plus times on simpler LEO missions now amortizes its fixed mass across a higher value,

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:42.380
multi-plane manifest. The next engineering checkpoint is whether the same stage can repeat the long

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:47.980
duration deployment sequence on a shorter pad turnaround, which would directly test the reuse

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:54.000
margin that has driven the half-year launch record. If battery thermal margins or cold gas reserves

00:07:54.000 --> 00:08:00.120
prove adequate, the architecture could support even denser rideshare manifests without requiring

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:11.280
a new upper stage variant. And a quick market note, SPCX is at $160.42 up 1.8%. Before we go,

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:17.140
Starbase hardware progress and Falcon reuse margins remain the clearest signals to track this week.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:28.420
That's your SpaceX news for today. SPCX is trading at $160.42. Up 1.8%. If the show saves you time,

